00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:04.030
JOE: Children and young people
with autism can be very intense

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:08.110
and focused about favourite toys,
activities and topics of conversation.

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:12.489
They may collect things and follow
repetitive actions when entering a room.

00:00:13.139 --> 00:00:18.169
Some individuals might have very narrow
interests or preoccupations, like needing

00:00:18.170 --> 00:00:20.350
to know everything possible about trains.

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:25.110
BETH: Sensory sensitivities can also lead
to developing obsessions and rituals.

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:29.220
Children might like to stroke people’s
hair whenever they can because they enjoy

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:31.650
the sensation, or it helps them feel calm.

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:35.789
Children with autism can get upset
when they can’t do their obsessive

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:38.110
behaviour, routines or rituals.

00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:42.640
JOE: Using obsessions or special
interests to help control behaviour can

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:44.620
make the experience more pleasurable.

00:00:44.929 --> 00:00:48.270
For example, when a parent gets a
young child to eat by pretending

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:52.019
the food is a train and their mouth
is a tunnel, this approach can be

00:00:52.019 --> 00:00:54.030
used with individuals with autism.

00:00:55.070 --> 00:00:58.720
By using their obsession or special
interest in something, creatively,

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:02.019
they can be encouraged to do something
they wouldn’t want too normally.

00:01:02.410 --> 00:01:06.350
BETH: Finding a positive outlet for an
obsessive interest, for example, if an

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:10.319
individual has an interest in dinosaurs,
they might be more inclined to go to a

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:14.610
museum to see fossils or eat food with
a link to dinosaurs on the packaging.

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:18.820
JOE: Also, obsessions and special
interests of individuals with autism

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:24.189
can be used as rewards and motivators to
help teach them new skills and behaviour.

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:28.860
If they complete a task or activity
or experience something new, they can

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:31.130
have more time doing what they love.

